BUILDING — onlyoffice-git
=========================

Updating to a new upstream release
----------------------------------

1. Bump `_oo_release` in `PKGBUILD` to the new DesktopEditors tag (`v9.5.0` ->
   `9.5.0`).
2. `./sync-submodules.sh --write` — resolves the ten module pins for the
   matching build tag and rewrites them. It refuses to write anything unless
   all ten modules carry that build tag, because a partly-published release
   would produce a tree upstream never built.
3. Re-read the three inputs whose versions are chosen by upstream's scripts
   rather than by the release tag:

   * **CEF** — `build_tools/scripts/core_common/modules/cef.py` selects a build
     directory by option; the default branch is the current CEF. At
     `v9.4.0.130` it is `5414`, i.e. `109.1.18+gf1c41e4+chromium-109.0.5414.120`.
     Update `_cef_url` and `_cef_build` to match.
   * **ICU** — `build_tools/scripts/core_common/modules/icu.py`. Keep
     `_icu_major`/`_icu_minor` in sync; the tag is `release-<major>-<minor>`.
   * **V8** — read the paragraph below, because this one is not a simple bump.
4. `makepkg -g` and replace the `sha256sums` array. Every pin is a `#commit=` or
   `#tag=`, so every entry is a real checksum of `git archive` of that
   fragment; there is no `SKIP`. A moved pin changes its sum and the build
   stops until the array is refreshed — that is the intended behaviour.
5. `makepkg --printsrcinfo > .SRCINFO`, then `bash tests/run-all.sh onlyoffice`.
6. A change to the patches alone is a `pkgrel` bump, not a `pkgver` bump.

The two version namespaces
--------------------------

They are not interchangeable and mixing them is the classic mistake here:

* `v9.4.0` — the DesktopEditors release tag. It exists **only** in
  DesktopEditors. `core`, `sdkjs`, and the rest have no such tag.
* `v9.4.0.130` — the build tag. It exists in each of the ten **module**
  repositories and nowhere else.

`pkgver()` reads `core`, not DesktopEditors, because only the modules carry the
build number. Pinning `core` by commit does not defeat this: the pin is the
tag's commit, so `git describe --tags --abbrev=0` still reports `v9.4.0.130`.
`prepare()` asserts that every module describes to the same build tag, so a
half-applied update fails before the build starts.

Ten sources, two origins
------------------------

`.gitmodules` at `v9.4.0` declares **six** submodules: `core`, `desktop-apps`,
`desktop-sdk`, `sdkjs`, `dictionaries`, and `web-apps`. The remaining four —
`build_tools`, `core-fonts`, `document-templates`, `sdkjs-forms` — are fetched
by upstream's own automation and are not submodules at all. All ten are pinned
here, which is what gives `no-git-update.diff` something to protect: upstream's
`git_update()` is disabled so it cannot re-clone over the pins.

`web-apps` is a rename, not a divergence: the *path* must be `web-apps` because
that is the directory `build_tools` addresses, while the *repository* upstream
declares is `web-apps-pro`. Both resolve the build tag to the same commit.

Why the pins are commits rather than tags
-----------------------------------------

`makepkg` derives a checksum for a `#tag=` or `#commit=` fragment by hashing
`git archive` of the fragment, so either form is verified. Commits are used
because 130-odd tags exist per module and a commit is what the release actually
recorded, so the pin cannot be re-pointed by a tag move.

Why V8 is not a normal pin
--------------------------

Upstream does not pin V8. `build_tools/scripts/core_common/modules/v8_89.py`
line 236 selects a **moving branch-head** — `remotes/branch-heads/8.9`, or
`…/9.9` on macOS — and runs `gclient sync -r` against it. That branch advances,
so an unmodified build is not reproducible and silently picks up new V8.

This recipe pins the commit `_v8_commit`, which is the tip of
`_v8_branch_head` (`branch-heads/9.0`), recorded alongside `_v8_tag`
(`9.0.257.43`) — verified by `git ls-remote`. Two consequences:

* The pin follows `branch-heads/9.0` while the script on Linux would ask for
  `branch-heads/8.9`. The 9.0 line is the later long-term one; 8.9 is what the
  script nominates. This deviation is deliberate and is what builds cleanly.
* To bump V8, resolve the tip of the recorded branch-head, find the `X.Y.Z` tag
  that points at the same commit, and update `_v8_commit`, `_v8_tag`, and
  `_v8_branch_head` together. If a tip has no tag, say so in the commit message
  rather than inventing one.

`prepare()` asserts `v8` is at `_v8_commit`. Upstream's script deletes and
re-clones V8 unless `v8.data` holds the version string it expects, which is why
that sentinel file is written (`v8_version_1`).

Resource expectations
---------------------

This is an hour-scale build. Upstream's own guidance for `build_tools` is 40–100 GB
of disk, 8 GB of RAM, and "hours even on a modern system"; upstream does not
support building against a distribution Qt, which is what this recipe does.
`archlinuxcn`, which builds this in CI with a maintained cache, caps itself at
two hours.

Do not run it concurrently with another heavy build. The binding constraint is
memory during the V8 and CEF links, not CPU: two simultaneous builds exhaust RAM
instead of queueing. Check `pgrep -x makepkg` first.

Known deviations from the archlinuxcn recipe
--------------------------------------------

* `java-environment=11` instead of `jdk11-openjdk`, so the requirement is
  expressed as the capability it needs. Only a JDK 11 satisfies `=11`, and
  `jdk11-openjdk` conflicts with neither `jdk-openjdk` nor `jdk17-openjdk`.
* `7zip` instead of the retired `p7zip`.
* `no-js-download-at-build-time.diff` is not vendored: upstream's copy is
  0 bytes, so it changes nothing while costing a maintained checksum.
* `onlyoffice.github.io` and `onlyoffice.github.io`-style unpinned sources are
  pinned here; upstream leaves the former on a branch.
* `sync-submodules.sh` here resolves all ten modules from the build tag using
  `git ls-remote` and needs no clone. The archlinuxcn equivalent clones
  DesktopEditors and reads `git submodule status`, which only ever sees the six
  real submodules and therefore leaves the other four stale.
